diff --git a/hserv/synchronisation/README.md b/hserv/synchronisation/README.md
index 8ba0ca7..9901ef5 100644
--- a/hserv/synchronisation/README.md
+++ b/hserv/synchronisation/README.md
@@ -58,3 +58,300 @@ remapping, dependency transfer and record-content upload. New satellite terms
 are accepted only below an existing master vocabulary. Structure remains under
 master control and is refreshed on every synchronisation. Local files are
 currently limited to 20 MB each and 40 MB per dependency batch.
+
+---
+
+## Review of 20 September 2026: current behaviour and safety contract
+
+The original description above is retained verbatim. This section is the more
+detailed description of the reviewed implementation, based on h7dev commit
+`5940d87` ("Revision of master-satellite synchronisation now bidirectional").
+
+**Historical text flagged for correction, not removed:** the earlier bullet
+"master-to-satellite change delivery and completion cursors" under "Deliberately
+not yet enabled" is obsolete: master-to-satellite delivery is implemented. The
+older description of delivery as incremental also needs qualification: this
+review deliberately uses history reconciliation on every run for correctness,
+while retaining bounded pages within a run. Saved cursors describe progress;
+they are no longer a permanent exclusion boundary. The original explanations in
+the interfaces are retained; counts labelled "updated" can therefore include
+unchanged records being rechecked.
+
+### What is and is not synchronised
+
+The master controls the shared database structure. A satellite contributes its
+own completed records, permitted vocabulary extensions and the files referenced
+by its contributed records. It can subsequently contribute additions to those
+same records. A satellite also receives master-originated records and records
+contributed by other satellites. This is not a general multi-master merge.
+
+Record IDs become canonical master IDs. Definition IDs and uploaded-file IDs do
+not have to match: concept identities translate types/fields/terms, and file maps
+translate uploaded-file references. The original implementation also retains
+same-numeric-ID plus exact-name/label compatibility fallbacks for cloned
+structures. These are weaker than provenance and do not establish that arbitrary
+independent databases have equivalent definitions. Use a shared, verified
+structure; resolve ambiguous definition identities before synchronising.
+
+**Record contents are additions-only.** A received snapshot is evidence of
+values to add, never authority to remove values already held by the recipient.
+Neither application direction deletes a record or deletes/replaces its existing
+`recDetails` rows. In particular, a missing image in an incoming snapshot leaves
+the recipient's image detail, detail ID and uploaded-file reference intact.
+Record-ID remapping changes identity columns and pointer targets while preserving
+the record and its values; it is not a delete-and-recreate operation.
+
+This contract concerns record data. Master-controlled definition and vocabulary
+metadata can still be updated, as before. An existing field's storage type cannot
+be changed by synchronisation, because that could invalidate its stored values.
+
+Existing non-empty title, URL and scratchpad headers are preserved. Empty
+headers may be filled. New records and verified temporary reservations receive
+incoming header data; established record type and ownership are not silently
+reassigned. Added details update the modification timestamp. Existing titles are
+not automatically recomputed as a conflict-resolution policy.
+
+Matching detail values includes their field, text, file reference, geometry,
+certainty, annotation and public-visibility metadata. Repeats are treated as a
+multiset: if two identical occurrences arrive and one already exists, one is
+added. Replaying the same two occurrences adds nothing. A shorter subsequent
+snapshot does not reduce the count. This cannot distinguish independently added
+identical occurrences across databases; global per-value lineage is not yet
+implemented. Different values are retained together, even if that requires later
+human reconciliation for a logically single-valued field. Changed metadata is
+also a new occurrence, not permission to overwrite the old one.
+
+### End-to-end sequence
+
+1. **Configuration and access.** The feature must be enabled on the server.
+   Both databases must be registered. Local operations require database-admin
+   access. The master verifies the enabled satellite's derived shared key, the
+   request action/body HMAC, timestamp and unused nonce. HTTPS protects transport.
+   Browser configuration responses omit the derived keys.
+2. **Local exclusion and preparation.** A database-level named lock prevents two
+   synchronisation runs on one satellite. The same lock serialises master sync
+   requests, manual sync remaps and configuration changes. Ordinary Heurist edits
+   are not globally locked by this mechanism. Private `sysSync` tables and the
+   journal triggers are installed before data transactions. Required data tables
+   must use InnoDB; no automatic engine conversion is attempted.
+3. **Inventory and discovery.** On first use, completed records predating the
+   journal are seeded once. Later runs scan the journal from zero through the
+   visible upper bound. Already allocated records and verified downloaded records
+   are excluded from new-record discovery. Previously uploaded locally owned
+   records are rechecked for additions. This deliberately catches transactions
+   whose journal IDs were allocated early but committed late.
+4. **Start or resume a master session.** The master returns the latest unfinished
+   session for that satellite, or creates one. A session identifies an exchange,
+   not record ownership. Permanent ownership comes from the satellite/database
+   and original-record mapping, so later sessions may update the same records.
+5. **Structure preflight.** The satellite describes the record types and populated
+   fields used by pending records. Unsupported types/fields are reported with
+   the existing master-template import guidance. Records depending on blocked
+   local targets are held back too. Compatible records continue; completion may
+   say that user action is still needed.
+6. **Reserve permanent IDs.** The master reuses a permanent mapping when one
+   already exists. Otherwise it creates a real hidden temporary master record
+   and stores the mapping in the same transaction. A lost response can therefore
+   be retried without allocating a second master record. The satellite saves the
+   response as `RESERVED` before changing local IDs.
+7. **Remap locally.** All old IDs first move to a disjoint high staging range;
+   then all staged IDs move to master IDs. In each phase *all parent Records IDs*
+   move first, followed by pointer values and audited references. This is needed
+   because core detail triggers can immediately write foreign-keyed `recLinks`.
+   The audited references cover details, links, dates, forwarding, comments,
+   archives, bookmarks, tags, permissions, reminders, working subsets and sync
+   state. Unknown ID-bearing columns stop the operation. Both phases and the
+   transition from `RESERVED` to `ALLOCATED` commit together.
+8. **Export a consistent batch.** The satellite reads file/term dependencies and
+   full record snapshots in one repeatable-read transaction. This prevents a
+   newly added file reference from appearing in content after dependencies were
+   read from an earlier database state. SQL snapshots do not freeze externally
+   modified filesystem bytes; file hashes validate the bytes actually read.
+9. **Send dependencies, then content.** Records are sent in groups of 100.
+   Satellite vocabulary extensions must resolve beneath existing master
+   vocabularies. Local bytes are base64 encoded and SHA-256 checked. The master
+   commits dependency registrations before accepting references to them. Content
+   validation resolves every field, term, file and resource target. The master
+   then appends missing detail occurrences and commits the payload receipt as
+   `APPLIED` in the same transaction. Only a successful acknowledgement changes
+   local outbound state to `UPLOADED`.
+10. **Read the master.** After pushing, the satellite starts a reconciliation
+    pass from journal position zero. Each master response takes a consistent
+    database snapshot and supplies at most 100 eligible records, ordered by
+    their latest visible event. This avoids repeatedly sending one record for
+    each of its individual detail events. The first page also compares structure
+    hashes. A changed structure includes base definitions and the vocabulary
+    snapshot, followed by record structures and mapped references.
+11. **Apply a received batch.** Within one satellite transaction the order is:
+    base definitions; terms; record structures; verified pointer-target shells;
+    file registrations/maps; all record headers; translated additive details.
+    Temporary pointer shells permit references to records delivered in later
+    pages. They remain temporary until their full record arrives. An occupied
+    incoming ID is accepted only when inbound provenance or an existing completed
+    outbound allocation proves it is the same master identity and its type agrees.
+12. **Checkpoint and finish.** The local data transaction must commit before
+    structure hashes or download positions are saved. Configuration updates use
+    a locked read/merge and a flushed temporary sibling followed by atomic rename.
+    Failure before saving the checkpoint leaves an older checkpoint, resulting in
+    safe replay. The progress log records success, failure or pending user action.
+    The current protocol does not have a master-side completion handshake that
+    transitions sessions to `COMPLETED`; a subsequent run can resume that session.
+
+### Interruption and retry behaviour
+
+| Interruption point | Committed state | Next run |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Before master ID reservation commits | No new mapping or reservation | Reserve normally |
+| After reservation commits but response is lost | Mapping and temporary master record exist | Same original identity gets the same master ID |
+| After local reservation save, before remap | Original record still exists; state is RESERVED | Resume two-stage remap |
+| During remap | InnoDB rolls back IDs, references and allocation status | Retry remap; never infer completion from half a move |
+| After dependency commit, before content | Terms/files can exist without record references | Reuse their mappings; apply record content later |
+| During content application | Entire batch, including its SQL receipt/provenance, rolls back | Retry without removing prior values |
+| Commit succeeds but response is lost | Committed data/receipt remain | Master receipt and additive matching prevent duplicate application |
+| Commit outcome is uncertain | Caller reports failure, advances no cursor, and retains file bytes | Retry and reconcile against committed mappings |
+| Data committed, checkpoint write fails | Data remain; checkpoint is old | Replay safely |
+| During file write or after DB rollback | An unreferenced attempt file can remain | Existing registered files are untouched; orphan cleanup is separate |
+
+There is deliberately no all-databases transaction. Earlier successfully committed
+batches remain committed when a later batch fails. This is resumability, not
+whole-run rollback. `SyncTransaction` checks commit results, rejects nested
+transactions and restores the sync-journal guard in `finally`. Normal Heurist
+triggers continue to run. A terminated connection causes InnoDB to roll back its
+uncommitted transaction, but cannot undo earlier committed batches or file writes.
+
+A sync lock serialises sync traffic, not all application writes. Row locks and
+foreign keys protect individual SQL operations. The record-ID staging range is
+locked before remapping. For a first deployment and for record-ID remaps, use a
+quiet maintenance window: an already-open edit form can still submit a stale old
+ID after a successful renumbering. That application-wide stale-client problem is
+not solved by a sync-local lock. Do not run schema changes, repair scripts or
+manual record deletion concurrently with the test synchronisation.
+
+### Files and image references
+
+Files are written with exclusive creation to a fresh random attempt filename.
+Every byte is checked, flushed and, where supported, fsynced before the SQL
+registration is inserted. No failed/uncertain transaction unlinks attempt files:
+a lost COMMIT response must not lead to deletion of bytes that were actually
+committed. The trade-off is possible orphan files, never deliberate removal of a
+previously registered upload. Review orphans separately; do not delete them merely
+because a sync attempt reported failure.
+
+A source file ID already mapped with a different content hash causes a stop for
+reconciliation; existing bytes and registrations are preserved. Updating a file
+in place, propagating external URL/metadata changes, and file-version merging are
+not implemented. Inbound reuse by existing MD5 checksum or external URL is
+retained from the original implementation; this is file reuse, not proof of
+record identity. The original direct-transfer limits remain 20 MB per local file
+and 40 MB per exported batch. The client/controller transport limits also apply.
+
+The image in the reported screenshot still exists physically, but no record
+references it. Before this review, both content-application paths executed
+`DELETE FROM recDetails WHERE dtl_RecID=...` before inserting the incoming
+snapshot. A snapshot omitting an existing image could therefore remove its link
+without deleting the image file, even on a successful run. That destructive path
+has been removed. The screenshot alone cannot establish which record was changed
+or whether a whole record disappeared; confirming the specific event requires
+record IDs, archive entries or a pre-sync backup. These changes do not reconstruct
+values already lost by an earlier run.
+
+### Configuration, identity and upgrading existing test databases
+
+Configuration belongs to one registered local database, role and registered
+master identity. The normal save path refuses to change those identities while
+retaining the configuration. Endpoint/name changes and secret rotation for the
+same registered identity remain possible. A copied configuration with another
+local registration number is rejected. Repointing to another master is a data
+migration requiring explicit reconciliation, not a settings-only operation.
+
+The new `sysSyncInboundRecords` table records verified incoming master IDs. Older
+versions did not keep that proof. On an already-used satellite, an older downloaded
+record can therefore collide with a new incoming record even when its ID happens
+to match. **Do not clear the database, remove its file references, or automatically
+approve every equal numeric ID.** Compare the record and its type against the
+master, the permanent outbound maps, backups and prior sync history. Only after
+establishing that it is the same logical record may an administrator record that
+identity in `sysSyncInboundRecords`. A genuinely unrelated local record needs an
+explicit safe ID reconciliation. No automatic migration guesses these identities.
+Existing completed outbound mappings already provide proof for those records.
+
+`Records`, relevant `rec*`, `def*`, `usr*`, `sysArchive` and `sysSync*` tables must
+be transactional InnoDB tables. If a legacy nontransactional table is detected,
+sync stops with its name. Review/convert it separately with a backup. The check
+cannot assess arbitrary third-party trigger side effects outside these tables.
+
+### Current limits and deliberate trade-offs
+
+- Rechecking journal history and resending candidates costs more than a simple
+  high-water cursor. It avoids permanently skipping late commits. Large databases
+  need performance measurement before broad deployment; the 10,000-page limit
+  can stop a very large run. This is correctness-first reconciliation, not a
+  claim of optimised incremental transfer.
+- Existing direction/ownership rules are retained. A satellite's own contributed
+  records are excluded from its master download. Edits made on the master to
+  those records do not currently return to that contributing satellite. Likewise,
+  local edits to a record received from another contributor are not uploaded as
+  that contributor's changes. Full shared-record editing needs a separate design.
+- There is no deletion propagation, priority-based winner selection, three-way
+  merge or per-detail origin/conflict resolution. Configured priorities do not
+  authorise replacing record data.
+- Empty incoming details do not clear anything. Conflicting values may accumulate.
+  Preserve first, then reconcile explicitly; this is the requested additions-only
+  policy rather than a conventional replace-with-latest snapshot.
+- Structure is still master-controlled. Field storage-type changes now stop,
+  but changing constraints, record structures or term labels can change how
+  retained data is displayed. Structure review remains necessary.
+- Direct SQL sync writes rely on Heurist's installed database triggers for link
+  and date indexes. They do not invoke every ordinary record-save side effect,
+  external search indexing or client-cache refresh. Validate those integrations
+  on the actual servers.
+- No code review can promise protection against disk/database failure, outside
+  deletion, unsupported custom triggers or administrators changing IDs manually.
+  The implemented invariant is that sync itself no longer deletes/replaces stored
+  record detail values and stops on unproven incoming record identities.
+
+### Components and their responsibilities
+
+| Component | Responsibility |
+| --- | --- |
+| `synchronisationController.php` | Local-admin/signed-remote dispatch, feature checks, sync locks, diagnostic responses |
+| `SatelliteSyncService.php` | Satellite inventory, preflight, allocation/remap, upload, download and progress orchestration |
+| `SyncSessionService.php` | Master sessions, compatibility checks, permanent allocation and additive upload application |
+| `RecordIDRemapper.php` | Schema audit and atomic two-stage ID/reference remapping |
+| `SatelliteInboundService.php` | Master structure/dependency/content application on a satellite, with identity checks |
+| `SyncAdditiveRecord.php` | Shared no-delete detail writer, multiplicity-aware retry matching and conservative headers |
+| `SyncTransaction.php` | Checked transaction and journal-guard lifetime |
+| `SyncFileStorage.php` | Immutable, complete file writes without truncating old uploads |
+| `SyncMasterChangeService.php` | Consistent master download pages and structure-hash comparison |
+| `SyncRecordPayload.php` | Portable record snapshots with field, term and file identities |
+| `SyncDependencyPayload.php` | Term ancestors/inverses, file bytes and pointer-shell export |
+| `SyncDependencyService.php` | Master acceptance of permitted terms and mapped files |
+| `SyncStructurePayload.php` | Definition export, identity translation, structure application |
+| `SyncChangeJournal.php` | Guarded SQL triggers, first inventory, candidate discovery |
+| `SyncConfig.php` | Validated configuration, identity guard, key redaction, atomic checkpoints |
+| `SyncSchema.php` | Private tables and transactional-engine requirements |
+| `SyncAuth.php`, `SyncHttpClient.php` | Authentication/replay protection and HTTPS request/response handling |
+| `SyncFeature.php` | Experimental feature and satellite structure-editing gate |
+| `SyncProgress.php` | Pollable phases, counts and preserved step log |
+| `configureSatellites.php`, `synchroniseWithMaster.php` | Existing configuration and run/progress interfaces |
+
+### Validation performed and remaining deployment check
+
+The package includes executable regression fixtures under `tests/synchronisation`.
+PHP syntax was checked with PHP 8.3. The actual additive writer and transaction
+helper were exercised through an in-memory mysqli test double: omitted image,
+stale snapshot, repeat multiplicity, replay, conflicting headers, injected insert
+failure, commit failure, malformed payload and nested transactions. The config
+tests use real temporary files and test checkpoint/key preservation, identity
+change rejection, corrupt JSON and redaction. Immutable file writes are tested
+against real files. A separate MariaDB 10.11 bootstrap SQL test checks the
+parent-first two-stage sequence with foreign keys and a pointer-update trigger,
+including retention of an image/detail ID and rollback of a new image.
+
+These checks do not constitute a full Heurist/MySQL 8 two-server integration test.
+This workspace cannot open the database socket needed for that test, and no live
+user database was accessed. Before deployment, use backed-up copies of master and
+satellite: compare record counts and every original detail/file reference before
+and after; repeat the run; interrupt between reservation/remap/dependencies/content;
+retry; and verify links, dates, file visibility, structure, and expected holdbacks.
+Install the matching reviewed files on both ends before enabling the test run.
